diagnosis_text:
The user reported intermittent "blinking" connectivity affecting two adjacent hosts sharing a gateway. A full Phase A–D diagnostic sweep found no supporting evidence for this claim anywhere in the Kath4 topology.

Affected component: None identified. All subsystems are healthy.

Mechanism examined: Intermittent outages of the type described — a shared gateway going briefly unreachable and recovering — would leave at least one of the following traces: a flap-inducing process or script on a switch/router, carrier_changes counters elevated above the startup baseline on an uplink interface, kernel syslog entries recording link-state transitions, DHCP lease drops or renewal anomalies on the affected hosts, OSPF neighbor adjacency instability, or packet loss in a reachability sweep.

Localization: Every infrastructure device was inspected. The sshswitch and mswitch0 — the gateways for all host VLANs — carry only FRR and sshd with no supplemental scripts. All access switches (switch1–4) run only a bash init process. All real interface carrier_changes counters read 2, consistent with a single startup bring-up cycle. Syslog on the gateway devices is empty. OSPF is fully converged across all nine routers (17 routes each, all Full). The tc_snapshot shows only default pfifo_fast qdiscs with no shaping. The current reachability snapshot shows 0% loss on all tested host pairs.

Root cause: No fault exists. The reported symptom has no mechanism, no historical trace, and no current signal in the live topology.

Fix: No corrective action required. If the problem recurs, capture a ping flood during the outage window and collect /sys/class/net/*/carrier_changes immediately after recovery to provide time-stamped evidence for a subsequent investigation.
=== END ===